ntp 4.2.8p18 for OPENSTEP

Installed:
  /usr/local/sbin/ntpd, /usr/local/bin/ntpq, ntpdc, ntpdate, ntptrace, ntp-keygen
  /usr/local/etc/ntpd.conf        the configuration (copied from ntpd.conf.dist the
                                  first time; never overwritten)
  /usr/local/etc/rc.ntp           boot-time script, described below
  /usr/local/etc/rc.ntp.conf      its settings (copied from rc.ntp.conf.dist)

Setting the clock and starting ntpd at boot
-------------------------------------------

rc.ntp runs ntpdate to step the clock once, so that ntpd starts close to the right
time, and then starts ntpd.  The network must be up first, so call it from
/etc/rc.local (or from /etc/rc after the network is configured; with roboclient,
after rc.dhcp):

    /usr/local/etc/rc.ntp

ntpdate asks the first "server" or "pool" line in /usr/local/etc/ntpd.conf (set
RC_NTP_SERVERS in rc.ntp.conf to use another server); ntpd uses all of them.  If no
server answers, it prints an error and starts ntpd anyway, with -g so that ntpd can make
the first large correction itself once a server becomes reachable.  It prints:

    NTP: setting the clock from 0.pool.ntp.org (first server in ntpd.conf)...
    NTP: step time server 192.0.2.1 offset 12.345678 sec
    NTP: starting ntpd...
    NTP: ntpd started (pid 215)

or, when the clock cannot be set:

    NTP: ERROR - could not reach an NTP server; the clock was not set
    NTP: starting ntpd anyway; it will set the clock once a server answers

It returns 0 when the clock was set and ntpd is running, and 1 otherwise.  Run twice, it
reports that ntpd is already running.  pkg does not edit /etc/rc or /etc/rc.local.
